lines+=['','Token and exact-input counts stay separate by target and input group. The no-fragment arm uses128 rather than512 candidate slots, so it does not isolate suffix expansion from wider verification; a budget-matched comparison remains needed. Full per-record results and paired bootstrap intervals are in fresh_r2_score.json; uncertainty is limited to this small two-book/generator panel.','','## Why this differs from the earlier solvers','','1. Derive a residual-write metric from the same prefix\'s attention output and MLP down matrices.','2. Look up candidates using both its embeddings and an intrinsic MLP-only forward table.','3. Expand those proposed tokens into proper suffix pieces using the existing tokenizer, preserving a fixed512-slot candidate budget.','4. Let the same native prefix verify candidates against H using only its own reconstructed history.','','Opened development failures concentrated on word continuations. For example, after a reconstructed partial word, the hidden state ranked a completed word or semantic neighbor instead of the missing suffix. Fragment expansion addresses that token-boundary mismatch. Published detokenization work is consistent with this interpretation; see LITERATURE.md. It does not prove information loss or universal invertibility.','','There are zero fitting examples, optimizer steps and additional learned parameters. The two FP32 lookup tables are disposable caches computed from the prefix; they are not another trained checkpoint. Parameter identity/version checks reject ordinary in-place or replacement updates until rebuilt. The tokenizer cache is fixed metadata.','','## Costs and numerical contract','','| Target / inputs | Reconstruction ratio vs A1+A2 | Ratio with one prefix-table rebuild per record |','|---|---:|---:|']
